content <- '<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><style>body{font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;margin:30px;line-height:1.4}h1{color:#1B365D;text-align:center}h2.sub{color:#0F4C81;text-align:center}h2{color:#1B365D;border-bottom:2px solid #1B365D;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:20px}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:15px 0}th,td{border:1px solid #CBD5E1;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left}th{background:#1B365D;color:#fff}.meta td{background:#F1F5F9}</style></head><body><h1>VIRTUAL INTERNSHIP: R DATA ANALYST TRACK</h1><h2 class="sub">Week 4 Final Capstone: Comprehensive Data Analysis, Modeling & Presentation Report</h2><table class="meta"><tr><td><strong>Intern Name:</strong> Suraj Kumar Rana</td><td><strong>Milestone:</strong> Week 4 - Final Capstone Report</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dataset:</strong> Titanic Disaster (titanic.csv)</td><td><strong>Environment:</strong> RStudio Desktop (R 4.6)</td></tr></table><h2>1. Executive Summary</h2><p>This capstone report consolidates the complete four-week analytics lifecycle conducted on the Titanic dataset (891 records). It progresses from Data Preprocessing and Outlier Engineering (Week 1), through Exploratory Visualizations (Week 2), to Hypothesis Testing and Supervised Logistic Regression Modeling (Week 3).</p><h2>2. Analytics Workflow Architecture</h2><table><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Core Technical Tasks</th><th>Deliverable</th></tr><tr><td>Phase 1: Cleaning</td><td>Missing value imputation (Median Age, Mode Embarked), IQR outlier capping, Z-scaling.</td><td>Cleaned dataframe (891 obs, 0 missing).</td></tr><tr><td>Phase 2: Visual Intelligence</td><td>Gender breakdown, class survival stacking, age density curves, fare boxplots.</td><td>4 high-resolution ggplot2 artifacts.</td></tr><tr><td>Phase 3: Statistical Modeling</td><td>Chi-Square test, Two-sample t-test, 80:20 Train-Test split, Logistic Regression.</td><td>75.98% Accuracy, 68.97% Precision, 61.54% Recall.</td></tr></table><h2>3. Data Cleaning & Preprocessing (Week 1)</h2><ul><li><strong>Imputation:</strong> Age missingness (19.86%) addressed using median imputation (28.0 years); Embarked imputed with mode ("S").</li><li><strong>Outliers:</strong> Positive fare skewness capped via IQR boundaries [0, 65.6] using Winsorization.</li><li><strong>Encoding:</strong> Created Sex_Binary (Female=1, Male=0) and standardized numeric features.</li></ul><h2>4. Exploratory Data Visualizations (Week 2)</h2><ul><li><strong>Figure 1 (Gender Survival):</strong> Female survival exceeded 74%, whereas male mortality was over 81%.</li><li><strong>Figure 2 (Class Disparity):</strong> 1st Class passengers had ~63% survival vs <25% in 3rd Class.</li><li><strong>Figure 3 (Age Density):</strong> Survival peak for children aged 0-10, heavy casualties for adults 20-35.</li><li><strong>Figure 4 (Fare Spread):</strong> Drastically elevated median fare and high dispersion in 1st Class.</li></ul><h2>5. Inferential Statistics & Modeling Results (Week 3)</h2><table><tr><th>Hypothesis / Metric</th><th>Value / Result</th><th>Empirical Verdict</th></tr><tr><td>Chi-Square (Sex vs Survived)</td><td>X-squared = 260.72, p < 2.2e-16</td><td>Reject H0 (Gender determined survival).</td></tr><tr><td>t-Test (Fare vs Survived)</td><td>t = -7.47, p = 2.7e-13</td><td>Reject H0 (Survivors paid higher fare).</td></tr><tr><td>Overall Test Accuracy</td><td>75.98% (136/179 correctly classified)</td><td>Reliable generalization.</td></tr><tr><td>Precision / Recall / F1</td><td>68.97% / 61.54% / 0.6504</td><td>Balanced predictive stability.</td></tr></table><h2>6. Strategic Conclusions & Future Directions</h2><ul><li>Emergency protocol design and cabin structural architecture directly dictate survival chances.</li><li>Future advancements include ensemble classifiers (Random Forest, XGBoost) and name title interactions.</li></ul></body></html>'

